  "authoring_note": "All 15 scenario records are authored for this project (not copied from a textbook). The German is written for A1: short main clauses, present tense, one new idea per utterance. A human must still judge naturalness - see docs/assets_provenance.md.",
  "language_note": "German is the content the player is learning, so utterance_de, the option labels and the feedback stay German. The interface around them is Russian and comes from data/ui/lex01_ui_ru.json. This replaces the earlier decision to keep all in-game text German, which made the slice unusable for a beginner tester.",
  "gloss_note": "utterance_gloss_ru (and gloss_ru on a respond option) is the short Russian meaning the 'Подсказка · Перевод' button shows. It is authored by the agent, needs human review, and is hidden by default: an answer given while a gloss is open is recorded as assisted, not as unaided. Only the record with guided: true shows its gloss without being asked, because that record is the teach-before-test example.",
  "guided_note": "Exactly one day-1 record carries guided: true. LEX-01 must present it first on day 1, keep its objects in the authored candidate positions instead of shuffling them, and show its Russian gloss without a request. Every other record keeps the shuffling described in presentation_note.",
  "action_type_meaning": {
    "find_select": "The player points at or selects the correct 3D object in the room. Options carry a scene_object.",
    "bring_deliver": "The player takes one small object and delivers it to a person or place. Options are the reachable objects; the delivery target is named in the utterance.",
    "respond": "The player answers in German by choosing one of two sentences. Options carry no scene_object; correctness comes from the recorded scene_state."
  },
  "presentation_note": "An option id is the positional label of the authored display order: a = first authored option, b = second, c = third. The correct answers are spread over those positions (a=5, b=6, c=4), so no position is correct in half or more records. LEX-01 must additionally shuffle the visible option order at play time and resolve correctness by option id - never by index - so a fixed position can never become the strategy.",
  "distractor_note": "distractor_lemma_ids = union of the lemma_ids in the incorrect options MINUS the union of the lemma_ids in the correct option. It is therefore always disjoint from the correct answer, and an empty list is valid: in a ja/nein answer pair both sentences use the same lemmas and only the polarity differs (s13). CI asserts this rule for every record.",
  "context_note": "context names one reusable room layout. 'room' and 'kitchen' are labels for the same tiny preview scene for now; LEX-01 decides the final layout.",
  "repetition_note": "Lemmas used in two or more semantically different roles: tomate (bring target, respond about location), kartoffel (respond naming, bring target), tisch (find target, delivery place, location answer), schrank (find target, distractor, location alternative), kuehlschrank (find target, delivery place, respond about location, distractor), broetchen (bring target, distractor), fleisch (bring target, distractor), sofa (respond naming alternative, respond comparison subject, distractor), bett (find target, respond naming).",
